# Routing

The filesystem provides structure. `route.dart` provides the typed contract
and may own a platform-neutral Document binding. Platform files attach only
the behavior they own.

```text
lib/routes/posts/[postId]/
├── route.dart
├── page.dart
└── server.dart
```

## Shared contract

```dart
typedef Params = ({int postId});
typedef Search = ({bool preview});

final route = AppRoute<Params, Search, NoData>(
  params: const PathParams<Params>.schema(),
  search: const SearchParams<Search>.schema(
    defaults: (preview: false),
  ),
);
```

The compiler validates record field types and emits a client-safe tree plus a
server-only tree. It does not use annotations, parts, registries, or
`build_runner`.

## Platform behavior

- `page.dart` attaches a Flutter page.
- `shell.dart` attaches a Flutter navigation shell.
- `server.dart` attaches loaders, HTTP handlers, and server functions.
- `route.dart` may bind a Document directly through `document.dart`.

Generated route references encode params and search values through the same
typed codec used for matching.

Flutter navigation uses the registered page or shell binding's shared route
identity. Once that binding is in `AppRouter.routes`, destinations from its
neutral definition or another wrapper navigate locally through `go`, `push`,
and `replace`. Compiled routes preserve this identity. A separate definition
with the same pathname does not acquire the binding. Server destinations
without a registered Flutter binding use browser navigation; native platforms
report that the route has no Flutter page.

Search values use the complete `defaults` record when decoding fails. Set
`invalid: InvalidSearchBehavior.error` for strict URLs instead: Flutter sends
the parsing failure to the router error builder, while server dispatch returns
a controlled HTTP 400 without running the route or reporting a server failure.

## Browser URLs and handoff

Routed Flutter applications set `webPathUrls: true` on the composition root
`App` to install Flutter's official path URL strategy before `runApp`. This
remains early enough when modules are provided by a lazy iterable, and gives
one typed location the same pathname and search values in Flutter, the server,
and prerendered HTML. Omitting the option preserves Flutter's or the host's
existing URL strategy; set `webPathUrls: false` for an intentional hash-routing
host.

If the live browser URL differs from an SSG handoff, the client discards those
stale loads and Query data and loads the current location instead. This
prevents a static `/posts/42` snapshot from erasing
`?preview=true&tags=one` during Flutter startup. Odroe's native server and
documented Cloudflare assets support path URLs.

## Capability keys

Optional entrypoints extend a neutral route with typed `RouteCapability`
keys. Capability keys also use instance identity: keep one top-level `final`
key and reuse it when attaching and reading the capability. Its name is only
diagnostic, so unrelated entrypoints cannot overwrite one another merely by
choosing the same type and name. Replace old `const RouteCapability(...)`
declarations with `final`; constructing another key at the read site requests
a different capability. Attach values through
`capability.attach(route, value)` so the analyzer fixes the value type from
the capability.
